      "created_at": "2019-04-12T16:08:48Z",
      "updated_at": "2019-04-12T16:08:48Z",
      "body": "I see your point. And I think you are right that you don't want take away power from the _Journal Managers_. The idea for suggested wording is a good start I think (and I will include suggested wording in the plugin).\r\n\r\nBut I also think that suggested wording will become a problem as soon as a plugin is updated and the _Privacy Statement_ no longer reflects what the plugin does in some way. In such a situation the _Journal Managers_ have to realize that the plugin has changed in a meaningful manner. And I'm not sure what would be the best way for a plugin developer to notify _Journal Managers_ about changes like that. Especially when you have a multi journal installation where every journal has its own _Privacy Statement_ but updates are done by the administrator.\r\n\r\nWhat would you think about an optional approach? So you can decide as a _Journal Manager_ if you want plugins to be able to extend your privacy policy automatically. This will enable the _Journal Managers_ that want to have control about the wording not to use the text provided by the plugin. But on the other hand _Journal Managers_ that don't want / aren't able to formulate an adequate _Privacy Statement_ can go with the text provided by the plugin.",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "pin": null,
